    thinking: "**Defining Server Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the server-side code, specifically `server.ts`. I\'m breaking down the necessary logic: an Express server listening on port 3000 (host 0.0.0.0), Vite integration as middleware in dev or static file serving in production, and an API route `/api/analyze` to handle requests.\n\n\n**Considering Image Handling**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to handle image uploads, and it seems the user can provide either text, or images, or both! I\'m leaning toward using base64 encoding within the JSON payload. This keeps things cleaner than using multipart file uploads.\n\n\n**Refining Output Format**\n\nI\'ve been reviewing the Gemini API and confirming the image upload format using base64. Now, I\'m concentrating on crafting a bulletproof prompt to guarantee the exact Markdown output structure, as specified in the instructions. It\'s crucial the response follows the required format, even with text corrections, and I need to ensure that.\n\n\n**Translating the Prompt**\n\nI\'m now instructing the Gemini API to provide its response as specific Markdown, and in Korean. I will confirm that the markdown items are: 1. \360\237\217\233\357\270\217 \354\233\220\353\254\270 \354\240\225\354\240\234 (Original Text & Correction), 2. \360\237\223\226 \354\204\261\352\262\275 \353\247\244\354\271\255 (Biblical Reference), 3. \342\234\215\357\270\217 \355\225\234\352\265\255\354\226\264 \353\262\210\354\227\255 (Translation) with [\354\247\201\354\227\255] and [\354\235\230\354\227\255], and 4. \360\237\222\241 \353\254\270\353\262\225 \353\260\217 \354\226\264\354\233\220 \355\214\201 (Linguistic Insight).\n\n\n**Adding Pilgrim Meditation**\n\nI am now adding the final section to the output format: 5. \360\237\225\212\357\270\217 \354\210\234\353\241\200\354\236\220\353\245\274 \354\234\204\355\225\234 \353\254\265\354\203\201 \352\260\200\354\235\264\353\223\234 (Pilgrim Meditation). This guide offers short, warm advice in three lines or less, linking the historical and theological context to the emotions a pilgrim might experience when praying or meditating before a relevant church, tomb, or artwork.\n\nI\'m torn now about how to render the output! Should I parse and display styled blocks in the frontend, or simply render the full Markdown? Or both?\nI like the idea of section tabs *and* a single-page layout with cleanly formatted Markdown under deep slate colors.\nLuckily, the styling instructions mention using `react-markdown`, which settles this nicely!\n\n\n**Analyzing Output Strategy**\n\nOkay, so I realized `react-markdown` wasn\'t actually installed, which threw a wrench in the rendering plan. I got a little worried about React 19 compatibility. Now, I\'m thinking about requesting JSON from Gemini instead! By guaranteeing that the API returns the sections as JSON, I can create custom card elements to style them, use custom icons (like lucide-react), outlines, typography, and micro-animations. But can I even do this? The instructions require \"structured Markdown\"...\n\n\n**Refining Response Format**\n\nI\'ve decided to refine the response format and will now instruct Gemini to return a JSON object with specific fields. This JSON will include the original data elements and the raw Markdown, which addresses concerns about output presentation and user flexibility. Specifically, the JSON will contain data for: purified text, biblical reference, translation (literal and contextual), linguistic insight, pilgrim meditation, and the preformatted Markdown text. This will enable me to build a highly polished UI.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the full-stack server entry point `server.ts` to host our Express API endpoints, run our Vite dev server in development mode, serve the React app, and handle secure requests to the Gemini API utilizing `gemini-3.5-flash`."
